ok let's get into audience questions for  the week three questions every week  first one today is from Brian brian says  hey David what evidence leads you to  believe people are fundamentally or  generally good like you and Joe Rogan  both said on the Joe Rogan show well so  this is a very old philosophical  question a question that exists even  beyond philosophy are we inherently good  or bad what about our greedy instincts  what about our selfless instincts what  about evolutionary advantages that one  or the other types of behavior conveys I  don't know that I have anything  particularly new to add to it I'll just  give you my take on it I believe people  are generally what I would call good  despite admittedly selfish and greedy  instincts that many of us have there's a  few actual data points if you want to  look at it there are some pretty good  studies from Harvard and Yale about what  are called first instincts in a variety  of different tests and people's  instincts overall tend to be towards  cooperation even if selfishness is in  the background as a motive sometimes for  that cooperation and that brings us to  what I think are some of the more  compelling sort of data points on this  which are evolutionary advantages humans  that better cooperate historically have  done best so in a sense there has been a  natural selection towards cooperation  and people that are sort of open to  acting in a way that benefits their  group even if underlying that are  selfish you're self-centered  interests and I think one of the really  interesting things about this is a lot  of the history on the evolutionary role  of religion and although I am not  religious myself and I believe that  modern religion is rife with problematic  ideas dogmatic blindly dogmatic thinking  defenses of violence justified by sacred  texts I mean all the people who watch  the show know my position on this I  actually do believe that  there are many points in history where  religious belief conferred an  evolutionary advantage where if you were  not in the in religious group it was  less likely that there would be sort of  cooperative relationships with the  people that you were living with whether  it's in a village or wherever else and  that those people tended to not do as  well  so yes selfish instincts exist but over  time nature in a sense has selected for  cooperative tendencies even if those  tendencies are rooted in some long term  plan that is selfish in other words well  I will act in a way that is cooperative  because I believe that that will  actually better serve me personally in  the long run well you're still  cooperating in that sense there are also  studies on babies which have found that  at really early points where we can even  get a sense of what is going on in the  baby's mind babies have an instinct for  fairness over unfairness as soon as they  can recognize it and they seem to prefer  good over evil they don't like  unfairness in puppet shows at very early  ages they tend away from violence at  very early ages and in the end  statistically most people are not  violent people who are committing acts  of violence in the real world most  people are not criminals as defined by  society most people lie mostly only in  the sense of white lies people do not  like and react positively to news of  criminality or violence or war or  terrorism or conflict and certainly  they're not participating in it  themselves so of course reality is  complex evil exists to the extent that  that's what we want to call it  radicalization exists but I believe  people most people are fundamentally and  generally good it's been not only  informed by my personal experience but  also by all of the different data points  that I've mentioned here all of which  are widely available for you to find  disagree with me let me know are people  fundamentally not good are people  fundamentally bad or evil or violent or  whatever  I'd love to hear from you about this
 

